 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T16:45+08:00",
 "answer": "Probably a little better, but don't count on clean air yet. The official forecast for tomorrow hasn't been published, but the outlook is leaning toward improvement.\n\n**Where things stand now:** The haze continued this morning (Oct 17), but the 24-hour PSI in central Singapore dropped back to \"unhealthy\" after staying \"very unhealthy\" the day before. At 11am, the 24-hour PSI was \"unhealthy\" in every region: central 184, north 139, south 154, east 174, west 176. This is the 13th day in a row of unhealthy air since Oct 5, the longest stretch since the 2015 haze crisis.\n\n**Why it may get better:**\n- NEA expects showers over Singapore and parts of Kalimantan on Oct 18 and 19.\n- It also expects the winds to slowly weaken and blow from the southeast or south. That could cut down how much smoke drifts over from Kalimantan.\n\n**Why it may not:** NEA warned that \"some smoke haze from Sumatra may drift towards Singapore.\" Rain also tends to give only a short break unless it falls on the fires themselves or the wind changes direction for good.\n\n**What to check:** NEA usually puts out its next-day PSI forecast each evening, so tonight's update should give the official outlook for Oct 18. For short-term plans, NEA recommends watching the 1-hour PM2.5 reading on nea.gov.sg or the myENV app. Until then, it's sensible to keep limiting long or hard outdoor activity, especially for elderly people, children, pregnant women and anyone with heart or lung conditions.",
